We will create a folder named `my-app` and separate it into two folders: `client` (React) and `server` (Node/Express).

### Phase 2: Set up the Backend (Node & Express)

1.  **Create the server folder and initialize:**
    ```bash
    mkdir my-app
    cd my-app
    mkdir server
    cd server
    npm init -y
    ```

2.  **Install dependencies:**
    We need Express (server), Cors (to allow React to talk to Express), and Nodemon (for auto-restarting during development).
    ```bash
    npm install express cors nodemon
    ```

3.  **Create `server.js`:**
    Create a file named `server.js` in the `server` folder and paste this code. We will create a "mock database" using a simple JavaScript array instead of a real database like MongoDB or SQL for simplicity.

    ```javascript
    // server/server.js
    const express = require('express');
    const cors = require('cors');

    const app = express();
    const PORT = 5000;

    // Middleware
    app.use(cors()); // Allows React to fetch data
    app.use(express.json()); // Parses JSON bodies

    // Mock Data (The Dummy Data)
    let dummyData = [
      { id: 1, name: "Alice", role: "Admin" },
      { id: 2, name: "Bob", role: "User" },
      { id: 3, name: "Charlie", role: "User" }
    ];

    // Routes

    // 1. GET: Show all data
    app.get('/api/data', (req, res) => {
      res.json(dummyData);
    });

    // 2. POST: Create/Add new data
    app.post('/api/data', (req, res) => {
      const newItem = req.body;

      // Basic validation
      if (!newItem.name || !newItem.role) {
        return res.status(400).json({ message: "Name and Role are required" });
      }

      // Add ID
      newItem.id = dummyData.length + 1;

      dummyData.push(newItem);
      res.status(201).json(newItem);
    });

    // Start Server
    app.listen(PORT, () => {
      console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
    });
    ```

4.  **Update `package.json`:**
